Why Monocypher: small, single-file C99 with no allocation and no platform
dependencies, so the identical sources compile for the ESP32 (xtensa gcc 8.4)
and for the host tests (gcc/g++). Audited by Cure53 in 2020, findings closed
in 3.1.2; 4.x has since reworked the API, so 4.0.2 is post-audit code, which
is why the host tests run the RFC 8032 vectors against it. Its low-level API
(crypto_eddsa_check_equation + crypto_eddsa_reduce + streaming SHA-512)
verifies pure Ed25519 over an image streamed in chunks, which a 750 KB image
on a 320 KB-RAM device needs.

Why not the libsodium the framework already ships (ESP-IDF's "1.0.12-idf",
precompiled into framework-arduinoespressif32): its pure-Ed25519 verify,
crypto_sign_verify_detached, is one-shot and needs the whole image in RAM.
Its multipart API is Ed25519ph, a different scheme from the "Ed25519 over the
image" docs/openapi.yaml specifies. And it is a precompiled 2017-era fork,
so host tests would run a different libsodium build from the one on the
device. mbedtls in ESP-IDF 4.4 has no Ed25519 at all.
